/* Metrics for the bar — fonts and spacing, and deliberately no colours.
 *
 * narchy owns every colour in the bar, through two @import lines it keeps in
 * style.css. This file is imported alongside them for the things a palette has
 * no opinion about, and must stay out of their way: nothing in here sets a
 * colour, so the two can never disagree.
 */

/* One font, so the icons agree with each other.
 *
 * Stock names Font Awesome 6 Free and Brands after the text font, so every
 * glyph resolves on its own and lands wherever it first hits. bluetooth is a
 * brands glyph and arrives from a 400-weight face 10px wide; the power-profile
 * scales come from Free's 900-weight Solid at 25px; the wifi arc and the cpu
 * die come from a Nerd Font at 20-24px — all at the same font-size, on the same
 * bar. Name one font that has all of them instead, drawn to one design size.
 *
 * {{ waybar_font_family }} is that font: it carries every codepoint this bar's config
 * uses, which is more than AdwaitaMono manages — that one is missing the muted
 * speaker, the ethernet plug, the charging bolt, the headset and both outer
 * thermometers. It has to be a family the fonts role installs; see
 * fonts_enabled, and note this is a Nerd-patched family, not a stock one, or
 * there would be no icons at all.
 */
* {
  font-family: "{{ waybar_font_family }}", monospace;
}

/* Equal air on either side of every module.
 *
 * Stock hands `padding: 0 10px` to a list of fifteen module ids, and #bluetooth
 * is on none of them — the stylesheet predates the module, and the word appears
 * nowhere in it — so bluetooth sits flush against its neighbours while the rest
 * breathe. power-profiles-daemon is the opposite case, on the list and then
 * given another 15px on the right of its own. Between the two, the gaps down
 * the right of the bar ran from 21px to 38px.
 *
 * So name the eleven modules this bar actually has, rather than inheriting a
 * list written for a different one, and give them all the same. Waybar's own
 * `spacing` adds 4px between widgets on top of this; it is even everywhere and
 * not what was wrong.
 */
#custom-recording, #pulseaudio, #bluetooth, #network, #power-profiles-daemon,
#cpu, #memory, #temperature, #battery, #clock, #tray {
  padding: 0 10px;
}

/* The recording indicator pulses, because a record mark that simply sits there
 * is read as an icon rather than as a state — and this one is only on the bar
 * while it is true, so it has nothing beside it to be brighter than.
 *
 * Opacity, not colour. Red is what a recording light wants to be and this file
 * cannot say so: narchy owns every colour in the bar and this stylesheet stays
 * out of its way, which is the promise in the header. An animation on opacity
 * breaks none of it — whatever colour the palette hands this module, it is that
 * colour, fading.
 *
 * The module is hidden the rest of the time, so the animation only ever runs
 * while a recording does. GTK does not tick keyframes on a widget that is not
 * realised, which means no wakeup per frame on an idle bar.
 */
@keyframes recording-pulse {
  from { opacity: 1; }
  to   { opacity: 0.35; }
}

#custom-recording {
  animation: recording-pulse 1.2s ease-in-out infinite alternate;
}

/* Even air above and below, and less of it.
 *
 * Stock's 3px border along the bottom is inside the bar's height rather than
 * added to it, and the modules centre in the whole of that — so the row of
 * glyphs lined up with the middle of bar-plus-border while the border read as an
 * edge, which put 13px of air above the row against 7 below. Padding the
 * border's own width back does not fix it: GTK does not move the modules for
 * padding on the window. Dropping the width does.
 *
 * The width only, so narchy's border colour stays declared and simply goes
 * unused. This file has no business setting a colour, not even to none.
 */
window#waybar {
  border-bottom-width: 0;
}

/* And this is what actually decides how tall the bar is.
 *
 * `height` in config.jsonc is a minimum, not a height — the bar is as tall as
 * the tallest thing in it, and that was never the text. GTK gives every button a
 * min-height of its own (24px under Adwaita), so the four workspace buttons held
 * the whole bar open at 24 no matter what the config asked for: 30 gave 30, but
 * 24 and 21 both gave 24. The font is not the floor either, and is not the knob
 * — 11px text still measured 24 tall.
 *
 * Releasing the buttons lets `height` bind, and at 21 the row lands centred:
 * 9px above the text and 9 below, where before it was 12 and 10.
 */
#workspaces button {
  min-height: 0;
}
